We need to compute tax for 16 independent cases. The tax includes income tax under §1 and possibly excise tax under §3301 (if the person is an employer paying wages subject to FUTA). However, the excise tax is imposed on employers, not on individuals generally. The problem says: "The total tax of a person is the income tax imposed by section 1 plus the excise tax imposed by section 3301 (if any)." So for each case, we need to determine if the person is an employer under §3306(a) and if so, compute the excise tax (6% of wages subject to tax, up to $7000 per employee per year, with exceptions). Also note that the excise tax is imposed on the employer for each calendar year. So we need to compute both income tax and excise tax, sum them.

We have many cases. Let's go one by one.

We need to interpret the statutes. Note that for income tax, we need to determine filing status (married joint, head of household, single, married separate) based on definitions. Also compute taxable income: gross income minus standard deduction (if not itemizing) minus personal exemptions. But note: For years after 2017, the standard deduction amounts changed, and personal exemptions are zero for 2018-2025 (§151(d)(5)). Also §68 (overall limitation on itemized deductions) is not applicable for years 2018-2025 (§68(f)). So we need to know the year for each case.

We'll go case by case.

Case 1: Year 2020. Alice and Bob married since Feb 3, 2017. Alice files joint return with Bob. Gross incomes: Alice $103,272, Bob $10. They take standard deduction. So filing status: married filing jointly. For 2020, standard deduction: For joint return, basic standard deduction is $? According to §63(c)(2)(A): For joint return, basic standard deduction is 200% of the dollar amount in effect under subparagraph (C). Subparagraph (C) says $3,000 in any other case. But there is special rule for 2018-2025: §63(c)(7) says for taxable years beginning after Dec 31, 2017 and before Jan 1, 2026, paragraph (2) shall be applied by substituting "$18,000" for "$4,400" in subparagraph (B), and by substituting "$12,000" for "$3,000" in subparagraph (C). So for 2020, the "any other case" amount is $12,000. Then for joint return, basic standard deduction is 200% of $12,000 = $24,000. Also additional standard deduction for aged/blind? Not mentioned. So standard deduction = $24,000. Also personal exemptions: For 2020, under §151(d)(5), exemption amount is zero (since 2018-2025). So taxable income = total gross income - standard deduction = (103272+10) - 24000 = 79282? Wait: 103272+10=103282, minus 24000 = 79282. But they may have personal exemptions? No, zero. So taxable income = $79,282. Then compute tax under §1(a) for married joint: 
Tax brackets:
(i) 15% up to 36,900: 15% * 36,900 = 5,535
(ii) 28% on excess over 36,900 up to 89,150: 36,900 to 89,150. Here 79,282 is between 36,900 and 89,150. So tax = 5,535 + 28%*(79,282-36,900) = 5,535 + 0.28*(42,382) = 5,535 + 11,866.96 = 17,401.96. Approximately $17,402.
Now excise tax: Is Alice an employer? Not mentioned. But Bob earned $10, likely not an employer. So no excise tax. So total tax = $17,402. But note: The question asks "How much tax does Bob have to pay in 2020?" Bob is a spouse filing jointly; the tax is joint liability. Usually both are liable. But question says "Bob have to pay", likely the tax amount for Bob? In joint return, the tax is joint. So Bob's tax is the same as the joint tax? Typically each spouse is jointly and severally liable. So answer should be the joint tax. But careful: The problem says "Alice files a joint return with her spouse for 2020. ... Question: How much tax does Bob have to pay in 2020?" Since they file jointly, Bob's tax is the joint tax. So we compute joint tax. So answer for case1: 17402 (rounded to nearest dollar). But we need to confirm if any excise tax? No.

Case 2: Year 2016. Alice died July 9, 2014. Bob is surviving spouse? They have a child Charlie born 2000. Alice died in 2014. Bob furnished costs of maintaining home where he and Charlie lived from 2004 to 2019. Bob's gross income in 2016 was $84,356. He takes standard deduction. Need to determine Bob's filing status for 2016. Bob's spouse died in 2014. For 2016, two years after death? Section 2(a) defines surviving spouse as taxpayer whose spouse died during either of the two years immediately preceding the taxable year. So for taxable year 2016, the two years preceding are 2014 and 2015. Since Alice died in 2014, that is within two years preceding? "during either of the two years immediately preceding the taxable year" means the death occurred in the two calendar years before the taxable year. For 2016, the two preceding years are 2014 and 2015. Alice died in 2014, so qualifies. Also must maintain a home for a dependent child (son/daughter) and be entitled to a deduction for that child under §151. Charlie is son, born 2000, so in 2016 he is 16 years old. Bob must have furnished over half of cost of maintaining household. He did from 2004 to 2019, so yes. And Charlie must be a dependent. Is Charlie a qualifying child? Under §152(c), qualifying child must have same principal place of abode for more than half of year, be younger than taxpayer and less than 25? Charlie is 16, so under 25, and living with Bob. Also Charlie must not have filed joint return. So Charlie is a qualifying child. So Bob is a surviving spouse. Also Bob has not remarried (not mentioned). So Bob qualifies as surviving spouse. Then filing status: surviving spouse uses same tax rates as married filing joint. §1(a) applies to surviving spouses. So we use married joint brackets.

Now compute taxable income for 2016. Year 2016 is before 2018, so standard deduction rules are pre-2018. For 2016, standard deduction: For joint return (or surviving spouse), basic standard deduction is 200% of the dollar amount in effect under subparagraph (C). Subparagraph (C) says $3,000 in any other case. But there is also additional standard deduction for aged? Not. So basic standard deduction = 2 * $3,000 = $6,000. That is for 2016? Wait, the statute as given includes both old and new amounts. Need to use the correct amounts for the year. For 2016, the standard deduction was actually $6,300 for single? But here the statute is simplified. We must use the numbers in the statute. The statute gives §63(c)(2)(C) as $3,000, and (2)(A) as 200% of that for joint. So $6,000. Also personal exemptions: For 2016, exemption amount is $2,000 (since §151(d)(1) says $2,000, but note that there is phaseout for high income. However, the phaseout is based on AGI exceeding applicable amount under §68(b). For 2016, §68 is in effect (since it applies to years before 2018, and after 2017 it doesn't apply). So personal exemptions may be phased out. But we need to compute. Bob's AGI is $84,356. For surviving spouse, applicable amount under §68(b)(1)(A) is $300,000 for joint return or surviving spouse. So Bob's AGI is below $300k, so no phaseout. So personal exemption amount is $2,000 for taxpayer, and also for spouse? For surviving spouse, the spouse is deceased. Under §151(b), an additional exemption for spouse if joint return not made? But if taxpayer is surviving spouse, they are considered married for the year? Actually, surviving spouse status allows them to use joint return rates, but the spouse is dead. For personal exemptions, §151(b) says an additional exemption for the spouse if a joint return is not made and the spouse has no gross income and is not a dependent. But here, the spouse died in 2014, so for 2016, the spouse is not alive. I think the taxpayer is entitled to a personal exemption for himself only. Also additional exemption for dependents: For Charlie, who is a dependent (qualifying child), Bob gets an exemption for each dependent. So Bob gets personal exemption of $2,000 and dependent exemption of $2,000. But note: §151(b) says "an additional exemption of the exemption amount for the spouse of the taxpayer if a joint return is not made by the taxpayer and his spouse, and if the spouse, for the calendar year in which the taxable year of the taxpayer begins, has no gross income and is not the dependent of another taxpayer." Since Alice died in 2014, she has no income in 2016, but the condition "if a joint return is not made" - but Bob is not filing a joint return because Alice is dead? Actually, a joint return can only be made if both spouses are alive at the end of the year or if one died during the year and the surviving spouse hasn't remarried. For 2016, Alice died in 2014, so they cannot file a joint return for 2016. So Bob files as surviving spouse, which is a separate category, but not a joint return. So he could claim an additional exemption for the deceased spouse? The statute says "if a joint return is not made by the taxpayer and his spouse" - here, a joint return is not made, but the spouse must have no gross income and not be dependent. Since Alice is dead, she has no gross income, and she is not a dependent. However, the statute might be interpreted to allow an exemption for the spouse only if the spouse is alive? Typically, the personal exemption for a spouse is for the spouse who is living with the taxpayer or is deceased during the year. For a surviving spouse, they can claim the deceased spouse's exemption for the year of death, but not for later years. The wording: "An exemption of the exemption amount for the taxpayer; and an additional exemption of the exemption amount for the spouse of the taxpayer if a joint return is not made by the taxpayer and his spouse, and if the spouse, for the calendar year in which the taxable year of the taxpayer begins, has no gross income and is not the dependent of another taxpayer." This seems to allow an additional exemption for the spouse regardless of death? But typically, a spouse who died in a prior year is not considered a spouse for the current year. Under §7703(a), marital status is determined as of close of taxable year, except if spouse dies during taxable year, it's determined at time of death. So for 2016, Bob's spouse died in 2014, so at close of 2016, he is not married (since spouse is dead). But surviving spouse status is a special rule; the definition of surviving spouse in §2(a) says "for purposes of section 1", but for marital status, §7703 says an individual is considered married if spouse died during the year? Actually §7703(a)(1) says determination of whether an individual is married shall be made as of close of taxable year; except if spouse dies during taxable year, then as of time of death. So if spouse died in a prior year, then at close of current year, the individual is not married. So for 2016, Bob is not married. So the additional exemption for spouse under §151(b) likely does not apply because the spouse is not alive. Also note that the surviving spouse status for tax rates is only for purposes of §1, not for §151. So Bob's personal exemption is only $2,000 for himself. And dependent exemption for Charlie: $2,000. So total exemptions = $4,000.

Now taxable income = gross income - standard deduction - personal exemptions = 84,356 - 6,000 - 4,000 = 74,356.

Now compute tax under §1(a) for married joint: 15% on first 36,900: 5,535; then 28% on excess over 36,900 up to 89,150: 74,356-36,900=37,456; 28% of 37,456 = 10,487.68; total = 5,535+10,487.68=16,022.68. Rounded: $16,023.

Now excise tax: Bob is not an employer, so none. So total tax = $16,023.

But wait: The question asks "How much tax does Bob have to pay in 2016?" So answer for case2: 16023.

Case 3: Year 2017. Alice paid wages of $53,200 to Bob for domestic service from Feb 1, 2017 to Sep 2, 2017. Alice's gross income in 2017 was $921,324. She takes standard deduction. Need to compute income tax and excise tax. Alice is an employer? She paid $53,200 for domestic service. Under §3306(a)(3), for domestic service in a private home, the term "employer" means any person who during the calendar year or preceding calendar year paid wages in cash of $1,000 or more for such service. So since she paid $53,200 (cash presumably), she is an employer for domestic service. However, note that she may also be an employer for other services? Not mentioned. So she is subject to excise tax under §3301: 6% of total wages paid with respect to employment. But there are exclusions: §3306(b) defines wages, with a cap of $7,000 per employee per year (paragraph (1): that part of remuneration which, after remuneration equal to $7,000 has been paid to an individual, is not included). So for Bob, wages are $53,200, but only first $7,000 are subject to FUTA. So total wages subject to tax = $7,000 (since Bob is one employee). So excise tax = 6% * $7,000 = $420.

Now income tax: For 2017, before 2018, standard deduction amounts: For single? But we need Alice's filing status. Alice is presumably single? Not married? No info about marriage. She may be unmarried, so filing as single (unless she qualifies as head of household or surviving spouse). No dependents mentioned. So she is unmarried individual under §1(c). Also she takes standard deduction. For 2017, standard deduction: For unmarried individual, basic standard deduction is $3,000 (according to §63(c)(2)(C)). But there is also additional standard deduction for aged/blind? Not mentioned. So standard deduction = $3,000. Personal exemptions: For 2017, exemption amount is $2,000 (no phaseout? Her AGI is $921,324, which exceeds applicable amount for single? For single, applicable amount under §68(b)(1)(C) is $250,000. So her AGI exceeds $250,000, so personal exemptions are phased out. Need to compute phaseout. §151(d)(3): For any taxpayer whose AGI exceeds the applicable amount under §68(b), the exemption amount shall be reduced by the applicable percentage. Applicable percentage = 2 percentage points for each $2,500 (or fraction) by which AGI exceeds the applicable amount. For single, applicable amount = $250,000. Excess = 921,324 - 250,000 = 671,324. Number of $2,500 increments = ceil(671,324/2,500) = ceil(268.5296) = 269. So applicable percentage = 2% * 269 = 538%, but capped at 100%. So exemptions are completely phased out (zero). So personal exemptions = 0. So taxable income = gross income - standard deduction = 921,324 - 3,000 = 918,324.

Now compute tax under §1(c) for unmarried individuals. Brackets:
(i) 15% up to 22,100: 3,315
(ii) 28% from 22,100 to 53,500: 53,500-22,100=31,400; 28% of 31,400 = 8,792; cumulative to 12,107 (actually given: $12,107, plus 31% of excess over $53,500). Wait, the brackets are:
(i) 15% up to 22,100: tax = 3,315
(ii) 28% over 22,100 up to 53,500: tax = 3,315 + 0.28*(53,500-22,100)=3,315+8,792=12,107. So next bracket: (iii) 31% from 53,500 to 115,000: 115,000-53,500=61,500; 31% of 61,500=19,065; cumulative 12,107+19,065=31,172; that matches (iv) says $31,172 plus 36% of excess over $115,000 up to $250,000. Then (v) for over $250,000: $79,772 plus 39.6% of excess over $250,000. So we need to compute for taxable income 918,324. This exceeds $250,000. So tax = 79,772 + 0.396*(918,324-250,000) = 79,772 + 0.396*668,324 = 79,772 + 264,656.304 = 344,428.304. So approx $344,428.

Add excise tax $420, total = $344,848. Thus answer for case3: 344848.

But check: Is Alice considered an employer? She paid wages for domestic service, so yes. Excise tax = 6% of $7,000 = $420. Good.

Case 4: Year 2018. Alice and Bob married April 5, 2012. They have a son Charlie born Sep 16, 2017. Alice and Charlie live in a home for which Alice furnished 40% of maintenance costs, Bob the remaining 60%. Alice and Bob file jointly from 2017 to 2019. Their incomes: Alice $36,991, Bob $41,990 in 2018. They take standard deduction. Bob lived separately? Actually says "From 2017 to 2019, Bob lived separately." So they are married but living apart? But they file jointly. For joint return, they need to be married and both consent. They are legally married, so they can file jointly. The fact that Bob lives separately does not affect filing status as long as they are not legally separated. So they file jointly. Also they have a child Charlie. Charlie is born 2017, so in 2018 he is 1 year old. He lives with Alice and Bob? Actually says "Alice and Charlie live in a home... Bob the remaining 60%." So they all live together? But then says Bob lived separately? That's contradictory. Let's read: "Alice and Charlie live in a home for which Alice furnished 40% of the maintenance costs, and Bob the remaining 60%, since September 16th, 2017. Alice and Bob file jointly from 2017 to 2019. ... From 2017 to 2019, Bob lived separately." So it seems Bob contributes to the maintenance of the home where Alice and Charlie live, but Bob himself lives separately. So Charlie lives with Alice, not with Bob. But Bob is the father. For head of household or surviving spouse, etc. But they are filing jointly, so status is married joint.

Now compute taxable income for 2018. Year 2018 is within 2018-2025 period, so standard deduction for joint return: $24,000 (since 200% of $12,000). Personal exemptions: zero. So taxable income = (36,991+41,990) - 24,000 = 78,981 - 24,000 = 54,981? Actually 36,991+41,990=78,981. Minus 24,000 = 54,981. That's the joint taxable income.

Now compute tax under §1(a) for married joint for 2018. Brackets: (i) 15% up to 36,900: 5,535; (ii) 28% over 36,900 up to 89,150: 54,981-36,900=18,081; 28% of 18,081 = 5,062.68; total = 5,535+5,062.68=10,597.68. Rounded: $10,598.

Now excise tax: Not mentioned. So total tax = $10,598. But the question asks "How much tax does Alice have to pay in 2018?" Since they file jointly, the tax is joint. Typically both are liable. So answer is joint tax. So case4: 10598.

Case 5: Year 2023. Alice's gross income $54,775. In 2023, Bob, the son of her son Charlie, lives at her place, a house that she maintains. Bob has no income. Alice takes standard deduction. Need to determine filing status: Alice is presumably unmarried? No mention of spouse. She has a grandson living with her. She maintains the home. She may qualify as head of household if she maintains a home for a qualifying child or dependent. Bob is the son of her son Charlie, so Bob is her grandson. Relationship: For head of household, under §2(b)(1)(A)(i), a qualifying child of the individual (as defined in §152(c)). Under §152(c)(2), "child" includes a descendant of a child? Actually §152(c)(2) defines relationship: a child of the taxpayer or a descendant of such a child. So a grandson is a descendant of a child, so he is a qualifying child if he meets the age and abode requirements. Also §152(c)(3) age: must be younger than taxpayer and less than 25 years old at end of taxable year. Bob is presumably younger than Alice and likely under 25. So Bob is a qualifying child of Alice? But note: The definition of qualifying child says "an individual who bears a relationship to the taxpayer described in paragraph (2)" which includes a child or descendant of a child. So grandson qualifies. Also must have same principal place of abode for more than half of year. He lives with her, so yes. Also he must not have filed joint return. He has no income, so fine. So Bob is a qualifying child. Then Alice may file as head of household if she is not married, not a surviving spouse, and maintains a household (furnishes over half the cost). She maintains the house, so she likely furnishes over half the cost (since she lives there and maintains it). So she qualifies as head of household. Also note: For head of household, the taxpayer must be unmarried at close of taxable year. She is not married. So she is head of household.

Now compute taxable income for 2023. Year 2023 is within 2018-2025, so standard deduction for head of household: Under §63(c)(2)(B), it says $4,400, but substituted by §63(c)(7) to $18,000 for 2018-2025. Actually §63(c)(7)(i) says: by substituting "$18,000" for "$4,400" in subparagraph (B). So head of household standard deduction = $18,000. Also personal exemptions zero. So taxable income = 54,775 - 18,000 = 36,775.

Now compute tax under §1(b) for head of household. Brackets:
(i) 15% up to 29,600: 4,440
(ii) 28% over 29,600 up to 76,400: 36,775 - 29,600 = 7,175; 28% of 7,175 = 2,009; total = 4,440 + 2,009 = 6,449. So tax = $6,449.

No excise tax. So case5: 6449.

Case 6: Year 2017. Alice's gross income $6,662, Bob's income $17,896. They got married Feb 3, 1992. From 2004 to 2019, Bob furnished costs of maintaining home where he and Alice lived. In 2017, they file separate returns, both take standard deduction. So filing status: married filing separately. Alice's tax separate. Need to compute Alice's taxable income and tax. For 2017, standard deduction for married filing separate: Under §63(c)(2)(C) says $3,000 for any other case, but for married filing separate, there is a special rule: For a married individual filing separate return, the standard deduction is zero if either spouse itemizes? But they both take standard deduction, so it's not zero. Actually §63(c)(6)(A) says: In the case of a married individual filing a separate return where either spouse itemizes deductions, the standard deduction shall be zero. But here they both take standard deduction, so neither itemizes, so the standard deduction is allowed. For married filing separate, what is the basic standard deduction? Section §63(c)(2) says basic standard deduction is: (A) 200% of $3,000 for joint or surviving spouse; (B) $4,400 for head of household; (C) $3,000 in any other case. "Any other case" includes single and married filing separate? Usually married filing separate gets the same as single? But the statute says "any other case" meaning not joint, not surviving spouse, not head of household. So married filing separate is in the "any other case" category, so basic standard deduction = $3,000. Also additional standard deduction for aged? Not. So Alice's standard deduction = $3,000. Personal exemptions: For 2017, exemption amount $2,000. But phaseout applies if AGI exceeds applicable amount. For married filing separate, applicable amount under §68(b)(1)(D) is 1/2 the amount applicable under subparagraph (A) for joint return, which is $300,000/2 = $150,000. Alice's AGI is $6,662, well below, so no phaseout. So she gets personal exemption of $2,000 for herself. Also she may claim an exemption for spouse? Since they file separate, and Bob has income? Under §151(b), an additional exemption for spouse if joint return not made and spouse has no gross income and is not dependent. Bob has gross income $17,896, so spouse has gross income, so no additional exemption. So Alice's personal exemptions = $2,000 (self only). So taxable income = gross income - standard deduction - personal exemption = 6,662 - 3,000 - 2,000 = 1,662.

Now compute tax under §1(d) for married individuals filing separate. Brackets:
(i) 15% up to 18,450: 15% of 1,662 = 249.30. So tax = $249.30, round to $249.

No excise tax. So case6: 249.

Case 7: Year 2015. Alice and Bob married Feb 3, 1992. Alice paid each of her 87 employees $5,207 for work done in 2015. She paid each of her 70 employees $6,341 for work done in 2016. But the case is for 2015. Alice and Bob file jointly in 2015 and had no income. They take standard deduction. So they have no gross income? Actually "had no income" meaning Alice and Bob had no income from other sources? But Alice paid wages, meaning she is an employer, but the wages she paid are not her income; presumably she had a business? The case says "Alice paid each of her 87 employees $5,207 for work done in 2015." This implies she is an employer, but her personal gross income might be zero? Actually the question: "Alice and Bob file jointly in 2015 and had no income." So their gross income is 0. They take standard deduction. So taxable income = 0 - standard deduction? But standard deduction cannot reduce taxable income below zero; generally taxable income is non-negative. So taxable income = 0. Then income tax = 0. However, they may be subject to excise tax if Alice is an employer. So we need to compute excise tax for Alice as employer for 2015. She paid 87 employees $5,207 each. Under §3301, excise tax = 6% of total wages subject to tax (up to $7,000 per employee). Since each employee received $5,207, which is less than $7,000, so all wages are subject. So total wages = 87 * 5,207 = let's compute: 5,207 * 80 = 416,560; plus 5,207*7=36,449; total = 453,009. So excise tax = 0.06 * 453,009 = 27,180.54. Round to nearest dollar: $27,181. But wait: Also note that these employees: are they performing employment? Need to check if they are in excluded categories? The work done is not specified. But likely general employment. Also note that the definition of employer under §3306(a)(1): any person who paid wages of $1,500 or more in the calendar year. Since Alice paid $453,009, she is an employer. Also must check if any of the services are excluded under §3306(c). Not mentioned. So excise tax applicable. But the question: "How much tax does Alice have to pay in 2015?" This includes income tax (0) plus excise tax = $27,181. But note: The excise tax is imposed on every employer for each calendar year. So Alice must pay it. So answer for case7: 27181.

But careful: The problem says "Alice paid each of her 87 employees $5207 for work done in 2015. Alice paid each of her 70 employees $6341 for work done in 2016." That second sentence might be additional info for the year 2015? Actually it says "Alice paid each of her 87 employees $5207 for work done in 2015. Alice paid each of her 70 employees $6341 for work done in 2016." So for 2015, only the 87 employees matter. So total wages in 2015 = 87 * 5207 = 453,009. No cap per employee? Under §3306(b)(1), the first $7,000 per employee per calendar year. Since each employee got 5,207, all is subject. So excise tax = 6% * 453,009 = 27,180.54 → 27,181.

Case 8: Year 2011. Alice and Bob married Sep 22, 2005. Their son Charlie born Jan 10, 2002. Bob passed away at the end of 2010. In 2011, Alice paid for her and Charlie's housing, a house that they shared. Alice's gross income was $25,561, she took standard deduction. Need to determine filing status for 2011. Bob died in 2010. For 2011, is Alice a surviving spouse? She meets the condition: spouse died during either of the two years immediately preceding the taxable year. Bob died in 2010, so for 2011, the two preceding years are 2009 and 2010, so 2010 qualifies. She must also maintain a home for a dependent child. Charlie is her son, born 2002, so in 2011 he is 9 years old. She maintains the home (she paid for housing). So she is a surviving spouse. So she uses married joint rates. Also note: She may be entitled to the standard deduction for joint? For 2011, standard deduction: For joint return, 200% of $3,000 = $6,000. Personal exemptions: For 2011, exemption amount $2,000 (pre-2018). She can claim personal exemption for herself and dependent exemption for Charlie. Also she may claim exemption for deceased spouse? For 2011, spouse died in 2010, so she is not married at end of 2011 (since spouse died prior year). But as a surviving spouse, she can still use joint rates. For personal exemptions, she can claim her own and dependent. The spouse exemption: Under §151(b), additional exemption for spouse if joint return not made and spouse has no gross income and not dependent. Since Bob died in 2010, he has no gross income in 2011. However, she is not filing a joint return (since he is dead, they cannot file jointly for 2011). So she could claim an additional exemption for the deceased spouse? The statute says "if a joint return is not made by the taxpayer and his spouse" - that condition is satisfied. And "if the spouse, for the calendar year in which the taxable year of the taxpayer begins, has no gross income and is not the dependent of another taxpayer." Bob is dead, so he has no gross income, and is not a dependent. So it seems she can claim an additional exemption for her spouse. However, note that the marital status at close of taxable year: She is not married (since spouse died in prior year). But the exemption for spouse is allowed regardless? In practice, the personal exemption for a deceased spouse is only allowed in the year of death. But the statute does not explicitly limit it to the year of death. The condition only requires that the spouse have no gross income and not be a dependent. Since the spouse is dead, he has no gross income. But is the spouse considered a "spouse" for the taxable year? Under §7703(a), marital status is determined as of close of taxable year, except if spouse dies during the year. Since Bob died in 2010, at close of 2011, Alice is not married. So for purposes of §151(b), "spouse" likely means a person who is married to the taxpayer at some point during the year? The statute says "the spouse of the taxpayer" without further definition. Usually, for tax purposes, the exemption for a spouse is only available if the taxpayer is married at the end of the year, or if the spouse died during the year. Since Bob died in a prior year, Alice is not considered married, so she cannot claim a spouse exemption. I think it's safer to assume no spouse exemption. Many tax texts indicate that a surviving spouse can claim the deceased spouse's exemption only in the year of death. So for 2011, only self and dependent. So personal exemptions: Alice (self) $2,000, Charlie (dependent) $2,000 = $4,000. Standard deduction: $6,000. Gross income: $25,561. Taxable income = 25,561 - 6,000 - 4,000 = 15,561. Then tax under §1(a) for married joint: 15% of 15,561 = 2,334.15 → $2,334.

No excise tax. So case8: 2334.

Case 9: Year 2013. Alice and Bob married Feb 3, 1992. They have child Charlie born Oct 9, 2000. From 2004 to 2019, Bob furnished 40% of costs of maintaining home where he and Charlie lived, and Alice furnished the remaining costs. In 2013, they file jointly and take standard deduction. Their incomes: Alice $71,879, Bob $11,213. So joint gross income = 83,092. For 2013, standard deduction: joint return, 200% of $3,000 = $6,000. Personal exemptions: For 2013, exemption amount $2,000. They can claim self exemptions for both (Alice and Bob) and dependent exemption for Charlie (since Charlie is their child, living with them? It says Bob and Charlie lived in the home, and Alice furnished remaining costs, so Alice may not live with them? Actually it says "Bob furnished 40% of the costs of maintaining the home where he and Charlie lived during that time, and Alice furnished the remaining costs." So Alice presumably also lived there? It says "the home where he and Charlie lived", not clear if Alice lived there. But they are married and filing jointly, so they are a family. It might be that Alice also lived there, but she contributed 60% of costs. So Charlie is their son, so he is a qualifying child of both. So they can claim dependent exemption for Charlie. So total personal exemptions: 3 * $2,000 = $6,000. But note phaseout: Their AGI is 83,092. For joint return, the applicable amount under §68(b)(1)(A) is $300,000. So well below, no phaseout. So taxable income = 83,092 - 6,000 (standard) - 6,000 (exemptions) = 71,092. Now compute tax under §1(a) for married joint: Brackets: 15% up to 36,900: 5,535; then 28% on excess from 36,900 to 89,150: 71,092-36,900=34,192; 28% of 34,192 = 9,573.76; total = 5,535+9,573.76=15,108.76. Rounded: $15,109.

No excise tax. So case9: 15109.

Case 10: Year 2018. Bob is Charlie and Dorothy's son, born April 15, 2015. Alice married Charlie on August 8, 2018. Their gross incomes: Alice $324,311, Charlie $414,231. Alice, Bob and Charlie have the same principal place of abode in 2018. Alice and Charlie file jointly, take standard deduction. So they are married filing jointly. For 2018, standard deduction = $24,000. Personal exemptions = 0. So taxable income = 324,311 + 414,231 = 738,542; minus 24,000 = 714,542. Compute tax under §1(a) for married joint. Brackets: up to 36,900: 5,535; 36,900-89,150: 28% on 52,250? Actually need to compute progressively:
- 15% on 36,900 = 5,535
- 28% on next 52,250 (89,150-36,900) = 14,630 (since 0.28*52,250 = 14,630) -> cumulative 20,165 (matches (iii) $20,165)
- 31% on next 50,850 (140,000-89,150) = 15,763.5 -> cumulative 35,928.5 (matches (iv))
- 36% on next 110,000 (250,000-140,000) = 39,600 -> cumulative 75,528.5 (matches (v))
- 39.6% on excess over 250,000: 714,542-250,000 = 464,542; 39.6% of 464,542 = 183,958.632; total tax = 75,528.5 + 183,958.632 = 259,487.132. Rounded: $259,487.
Now excise tax? None mentioned. So case10: 259487.

But wait: Is there any dependent? Bob is son of Charlie and Dorothy, but Alice married Charlie, so Bob is Charlie's son, not biologically Alice's, but stepchild? Alice is stepmother. Bob is a qualifying child? For head of household, but they file jointly, so no. Also Bob may be a dependent? But exemption amount is zero for 2018. So no effect.

Thus case10: 259487.

Case 11: Year 2017. Alice's gross income $44,215. Alice and Bob married since Oct 10, 2017. They file separately. Alice paid $3,200 in cash to Bob for agricultural labor done from Feb 1 to Sep 2, 2017. Alice takes standard deduction. They live separately in 2017. Need to compute Alice's tax. First, filing status: married filing separately. Since they married on Oct 10, 2017, they are married at end of year. They file separate returns. So Alice is married filing separate. Standard deduction: For married filing separate, standard deduction is $3,000? But note that if either spouse itemizes, the standard deduction is zero. They both take standard deduction? It says Alice takes standard deduction. Bob might also take standard deduction. So both taking standard deduction is allowed? Actually §63(c)(6)(A) says: In the case of a married individual filing a separate return where either spouse itemizes deductions, the standard deduction shall be zero. Since both take standard deduction, neither itemizes, so condition not triggered. So standard deduction is allowed. For married separate, basic standard deduction is $3,000 (since "any other case" includes married separate). So Alice's standard deduction = $3,000. Personal exemptions: For 2017, exemption amount $2,000. She can claim her own exemption. Also may claim spouse exemption? Under §151(b), additional exemption for spouse if joint return not made and spouse has no gross income and not dependent. Bob has gross income? He was paid $3,200 for agricultural labor, so he has gross income. So no spouse exemption. So personal exemption = $2,000. So taxable income = 44,215 - 3,000 - 2,000 = 39,215. Now compute tax under §1(d) for married separate. Brackets:
(i) 15% up to 18,450: 2,767.50
(ii) 28% from 18,450 to 44,575: 39,215-18,450=20,765; 28% of 20,765 = 5,814.20; total = 2,767.50+5,814.20=8,581.70. So tax = $8,582.

Now excise tax: Alice is an employer? She paid $3,200 to Bob for agricultural labor. For agricultural labor, employer definition under §3306(a)(2): For agricultural labor, employer means any person who during calendar year or preceding calendar year paid wages of $20,000 or more for agricultural labor, or employed at least 5 individuals on some 10 days. Here she paid only $3,200, which is less than $20,000, and she employed only Bob (one individual), so not meeting the conditions. So she is not an employer for agricultural labor. However, there is also the general employer definition under §3306(a)(1) which applies to any person who paid wages of $1,500 or more. But note: For purposes of (a)(1), there shall not be taken into account any wages paid to, or employment of, an employee performing domestic services referred to in paragraph (3). Agricultural labor is not domestic service. So agricultural wages are considered for the general test? Actually §3306(a)(1) says "For purposes of this paragraph, there shall not be taken into account any wages paid to, or employment of, an employee performing domestic services referred to in paragraph (3)." It only excludes domestic services, not agricultural. So general test includes agricultural wages. So Alice paid $3,200 in wages for agricultural labor, which is over $1,500, so she is an employer under (a)(1). But careful: (a)(1) says "any person who during the calendar year or the preceding calendar year paid wages of $1,500 or more" - that includes wages for any employment? But there is also the definition of "employment" in §3306(c). For agricultural labor, it is employment only if it meets conditions in §3306(c)(1). Let's check: §3306(c) defines employment as any service performed by an employee for the person employing him, within the United States, except certain services. Agricultural labor is excepted unless it meets conditions in (c)(1). (c)(1) says agricultural labor is not employment unless (A) such labor is performed for a person who (i) during the calendar year or preceding calendar year paid remuneration in cash of $20,000 or more to individuals employed in agricultural labor, or (ii) on each of some 10 days during the calendar year or preceding calendar year employed at least 5 individuals in agricultural labor. So agricultural labor is only employment if the employer meets either the $20,000 test or the 10-day/5-employee test. Since Alice paid only $3,200 and employed only Bob, she does not meet those conditions. Therefore, the agricultural labor performed by Bob is not "employment" under §3306(c). Hence, the wages paid for that labor are not subject to FUTA. So Alice is not subject to excise tax on those wages. So no excise tax. So total tax for Alice = $8,582.

But wait: There is also the possibility that Alice is an employer under other provisions? No. So case11: 8582.

Case 12: Year 2020. Bob is Charlie and Dorothy's son, born April 15, 2015. Alice married Charlie on August 8, 2018. Alice was paid $73,200 in 2020 as an employee of the United States Government in Arlington, VA. She files a separate return and takes standard deduction. Since 2019, Alice, Bob and Charlie live in a house maintained by Alice and Charlie. Need to compute Alice's tax. She files separate return. She is married? She married Charlie in 2018, so in 2020 she is still married (unless divorced, not mentioned). She files separate return. So filing status: married filing separate. However, note that she may qualify as head of household? For head of household, she must be not married at close of taxable year. She is married, but there is an exception: §2(b)(2)(A) says an individual who is legally separated from spouse under decree of divorce or separate maintenance shall not be considered as married. Not applicable. Also §7703(b) allows certain married individuals living apart to be considered not married if they meet conditions: (1) files separate return, (2) maintains a home for a child, (3) furnishes over half the cost, (4) spouse not a member of household during last 6 months. Here, Alice and Charlie live together? It says "Alice, Bob and Charlie live in a house maintained by Alice and Charlie." So they all live together. So she does not meet the condition that spouse is not a member of household for last 6 months. So she is considered married. So she files as married separate. Also note that she is an employee of US Government. That income is gross income. She takes standard deduction. For 2020, standard deduction for married separate: $12,000? Actually for 2020, under special rule, basic standard deduction for "any other case" is $12,000 (since substituted for $3,000). So for married separate, it's $12,000. But careful: The special rule says "by substituting '$12,000' for '$3,000' in subparagraph (C)". So standard deduction for married separate = $12,000. So standard deduction = $12,000. Personal exemptions: zero (2018-2025). So taxable income = 73,200 - 12,000 = 61,200. Now compute tax under §1(d) for married separate. Brackets:
(i) 15% up to 18,450: 2,767.50
(ii) 28% over 18,450 up to 44,575: 44,575-18,450=26,125; 28% = 7,315; cumulative 10,082.50
(iii) 31% over 44,575 up to 70,000: 61,200-44,575=16,625; 31% = 5,153.75; cumulative 15,236.25? Actually the bracket (iii) says $10,082.50 plus 31% of excess over $44,575 up to $70,000. So tax = 10,082.50 + 0.31*(61,200-44,575) = 10,082.50 + 0.31*16,625 = 10,082.50 + 5,153.75 = 15,236.25. So tax = $15,236. Rounded: $15,236.

Excise tax: Not an employer. So case12: 15236.

Case 13: Year 2010. Alice paid $300 to Bob for domestic service done from Feb 1, 2010 to Sep 2, 2010, in Baltimore, MD. Alice's gross income in 2010 was $33,408. She is allowed itemized deductions of $680, $2,102, $1,993 and $4,807. So she itemizes deductions. Need to compute taxable income. First, determine filing status: No info about marital status or dependents. Assume she is single (unless indicated). She likely is unmarried. So filing status: unmarried